        title: "Module 1: Mine Safety Legislation, Duty of Care & Risk Management",
        durationMinutes: 45,
        sequenceOrder: 1,
        contentType: 'TEXT',
        contentUrl: null,
        content: `STANDARD 11 (MINING INDUCTION) — LEGISLATION & RISK MANAGEMENT

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
SECTION 1: THE LEGISLATIVE FRAMEWORK — COAL MINING SAFETY & HEALTH ACT
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

The Standard 11 (Standard for Mine Safety and Health Training) is the mandatory generic induction for surface coal mining operations in Queensland. It is driven by the Coal Mining Safety and Health Act 1999 (CMSHA) and the corresponding Regulations. The core philosophy of the Act is that the risk of injury or illness to any person resulting from coal mining operations must be at an "acceptable level."

The Concept of "Acceptable Level of Risk":
The Act recognises that mining is inherently dangerous and it is impossible to eliminate all risk. Instead, the law requires that risks be reduced to an "acceptable level." This means the risk must be within acceptable limits, and as low as reasonably achievable (ALARA).

Duty of Care (Section 39):
The CMSHA establishes strict, non-delegable health and safety obligations for everyone on a mine site. 
→ The Site Senior Executive (SSE): Has the ultimate responsibility to ensure the safety of the entire operation.
→ Supervisors: Have an obligation to ensure the safety and health of workers under their control, ensuring they are competent and following procedures.
→ Every Worker (You): Has a legal obligation to:
    1. Comply with the site Safety and Health Management System (SHMS).
    2. Take reasonable care for your own safety.
    3. Take reasonable care that your actions (or omissions) do not adversely affect the safety of others.
    4. Not willfully or recklessly interfere with safety equipment.

Refusal to Work:
Under the legislation, you have the absolute legal right—and the obligation—to stop work if you believe the task is unsafe or the risk is unacceptable. You cannot be penalised or dismissed for refusing to undertake an unsafe task, provided you immediately report the hazard to your supervisor.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
SECTION 2: THE SAFETY AND HEALTH MANAGEMENT SYSTEM (SHMS)
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

Every mine site must develop and implement a comprehensive SHMS. The SHMS is the "rulebook" for the site. It contains all the policies, Principal Hazard Management Plans (PHMPs), Standard Operating Procedures (SOPs), and training requirements.

As a worker, you must be trained in the specific SOPs relevant to your tasks before you are authorised to perform them. "I didn't know the procedure" is not a valid legal defence in the event of an incident.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
SECTION 3: PERSONAL RISK ASSESSMENT — THE TAKE 5 / JSEA
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

Risk management is not just for management; it is a continuous process performed by every worker, every day, for every task. The primary tools for this are the Personal Risk Assessment (Take 5) and the Job Safety and Environmental Analysis (JSEA).

THE TAKE 5 (Personal Risk Assessment):
Before commencing any task, every worker must conduct a mental and documented physical check of the immediate work environment.
1. STOP: Step back and look at the job.
2. LOOK: Identify the hazards (what could go wrong?).
3. ASSESS: Assess the risk (how bad could it be, and how likely is it?).
4. CONTROL: Implement controls (what am I going to do to make it safe?).
5. MONITOR: Keep monitoring the job. If conditions change, stop and reassess.

A Take 5 must be documented in your pocket booklet. It is a legal record showing you assessed the work area before starting.

THE JSEA / SWMS:
For more complex, non-routine, or high-risk tasks, a formal Job Safety and Environmental Analysis (JSEA) or Safe Work Method Statement (SWMS) must be completed.
A JSEA involves a group of workers:
1. Breaking the job down into step-by-step sequential tasks.
2. Identifying the specific hazards associated with each individual step.
3. Determining and documenting the specific controls for each hazard based on the Hierarchy of Controls.
4. Assigning responsibility to specific individuals.

Everyone involved in the task must sign the JSEA before work commences, confirming they understand their role and the required controls.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
SECTION 4: THE HIERARCHY OF CONTROLS
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

When you identify a hazard during your Take 5 or JSEA, you must apply controls based on the Hierarchy of Controls. You must always attempt to use the highest level control reasonably practicable.

1. Elimination: Physically remove the hazard (e.g., do the work on the ground instead of at height).
2. Substitution: Replace the hazard (e.g., use a non-toxic solvent instead of a highly toxic one).
3. Isolation/Separation: Isolate the hazard from people (e.g., install physical barricades around an excavation, or lockout electrical energy).
4. Engineering Controls: Physical changes to the work environment (e.g., installing local exhaust ventilation to remove welding fumes).
5. Administrative Controls: Rules and procedures (e.g., warning signs, restricted access zones, job rotation).
6. Personal Protective Equipment (PPE): The absolute last line of defence. Protects the worker only after the hazard has bypassed all other controls (e.g., hard hats, safety glasses, respirators).`
